# Reflection Journal — Jekyll Starter Kit

A beginner-friendly static journal website built with **Jekyll** and hosted free on **GitHub Pages**.
No comments, no login, no visitor interaction. You write Markdown files, commit them, and the
site rebuilds itself.

## What is in this folder

```
reflection-journal/
├── _config.yml        # Site title, description, theme, menu links
├── index.md           # Homepage: about this journal + list of entries
├── goals.md           # Permanent long-term goals page
└── _posts/
    └── 2026-11-16-welcome.md   # A sample journal entry (copy this for new ones)
```

---

## PART A — One-time setup on GitHub (about 15 minutes)

### 1. Make a GitHub account
1. Go to <https://github.com> and click **Sign up**.
2. Use an email you check, set a password, and verify the account.

### 2. Create a new repository
1. Click the **+** icon (top right) → **New repository**.
2. Fill in:
   - **Repository name:** choose one, e.g. `reflection-journal`.
     - If you want your main site at `https://yourusername.github.io`, name it exactly
       `yourusername.github.io` (replace with your real username).
   - **Visibility:** select **Public** (GitHub Pages free hosting needs a public repo).
   - **Add a README file:** leave unchecked for now.
3. Click **Create repository**.

### 3. Upload these starter files
You will put the four files from this folder into your new repo.

**Easiest way (drag and drop):**
1. On your new repo page, click **Add file** → **Upload files**.
2. Open this `reflection-journal` folder on your computer.
3. Drag the whole folder's contents into the upload area:
   - `_config.yml`
   - `index.md`
   - `goals.md`
   - the `_posts` folder (with the sample `.md` inside it)
4. At the bottom, under **Commit changes**, leave the message as "Add starter files", then
   click **Commit changes**.

> If drag-and-drop does not keep the `_posts` folder, create it manually:
> **Add file** → **Create new file**, type `_posts/2026-11-16-welcome.md` in the name box
> (the `/` makes the folder), then paste the content.

### 4. Turn on GitHub Pages
1. Inside your repo, click **Settings** (top menu bar).
2. On the left sidebar, under **Code and automation**, click **Pages**.
3. Under **Build and deployment**:
   - **Source:** choose **Deploy from a branch**.
   - **Branch:** choose `main`, folder `/ (root)`.
4. Click **Save**.

### 5. Wait, then open your site
- Give it 1–10 minutes for the first build.
- Your address will be:
  - `https://yourusername.github.io` (if the repo is named `yourusername.github.io`)
  - `https://yourusername.github.io/reflection-journal/` (any other repo name)
- Refresh until the page appears. If it shows a 404 at first, the build is still running.

### 6. Update `_config.yml` to match your real name and URL
Open `_config.yml` in the repo and edit:
- `author: Your Name` → your name.
- `url:` → your real site URL.
- If your repo name is **not** `yourusername.github.io`, set `baseurl:` to `"/reflection-journal"`
  (use your actual repo name).
- Commit the change.

---

## PART B — How to add a new journal entry (the only daily task)

Every reflection is one Markdown file inside the `_posts` folder.

1. In your repo, go into the `_posts` folder.
2. **Add file** → **Create new file**.
3. Name it strictly: `YYYY-MM-DD-short-title.md`
   - Example: `2026-11-20-biotech-project-reflection.md`
   - The date must be real and in this exact format, or the entry will not show up.
4. Copy this template at the top of the file:

```markdown
---
layout: post
title: "Reflection - Nov 20"
date: 2026-11-20
tags: [biotech]
---

## What I worked on today

-

## Progress toward my goals

- **Goal 1:**

## What was hard

-

## What I learned

-

## Next step

-
```

5. Write your reflection below the `---` line.
6. Scroll down → **Commit changes**. The entry appears on the homepage within a few minutes,
   newest first.

### Markdown quick reference
- `## Heading` → subheading
- `- item` → bullet list
- `**bold**` → bold
- `[link text](https://...)` → a link
- Leave a blank line between paragraphs.

---

## PART C — How to update your goals page

1. Open `goals.md` in the repo.
2. Click the pencil icon (**Edit this file**).
3. Change the goal titles, check off benchmarks with `[x]`, and update the **Last updated** date.
4. **Commit changes.**

---

## PART D — Important rules and limits

- **This site is public.** Anyone with the link can read it. Do not put private diary feelings,
  passwords, or personal details here. Keep it to school projects, learning notes, and goal
  progress. Save truly private writing in a private app instead.
- **No online writing box.** This is a static site: you cannot type into a form on the webpage
  and have it save. You add each entry as a file. (That is normal for this kind of project and
  worth explaining in a college interview.)
- **No comments, likes, or accounts** are set up, which matches what you wanted.
- Files must end in `.md` (Markdown) for posts.

---

## PART E — Troubleshooting

| Problem | Fix |
| --- | --- |
| 404 error after enabling Pages | Wait a few more minutes; check Settings → Pages shows "Your site is live". Make sure branch is `main`, folder `/ (root)`. |
| My new entry does not appear | Check the filename starts with a valid date `YYYY-MM-DD-`, and the file is inside `_posts`. |
| Site styling looks broken / plain | Confirm `theme: minima` is in `_config.yml` and you committed it. |
| Links between pages are 404 | Match `baseurl` in `_config.yml` to your repo name (see step 6). |
| Page updates slowly | GitHub rebuilds in 1–10 minutes after each commit; refresh later. |

## Optional later upgrades
- Install GitHub Desktop or VS Code to write entries offline, then push them up.
- Add a simple custom color scheme later once the basics work.
- Add tags and a tags page.
